---

<BaseLayout
  navKey="features"
  title="Features: GPU focus stacking, RAW & retouching"
  description="Pyramid, Depth, and Wavelet GPU focus stacking, automatic alignment, linear RAW to 16-bit DNG, non-destructive retouching, batch processing, and CLI."
  bodyClass="features-page"
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">macOS (Apple silicon) · Windows · Linux preview · iPhone &amp; iPad soon</p>
      <h1>Features</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      GPU-accelerated focus stacking with three algorithms, precision alignment, linear RAW and DNG support,
      non-destructive retouching, batch processing, and a full command-line tool.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="glance-strip" id="spec" aria-label="At a glance">
    <dl>
      <div>
        <dt>Stacking &amp; Alignment</dt>
        <dd><strong>Pyramid, Depth, Wavelet</strong> with automatic ECC affine or Grid search alignment.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>RAW &amp; Color Pipeline</dt>
        <dd><strong>32-bit float</strong> in linear camera RGB. Camera RAW in, <strong>16-bit linear DNG</strong>, TIFF, PNG, or JPEG out.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Workflow &amp; Automation</dt>
        <dd><strong>Non-destructive retouching</strong>, 100% split comparison, Library history, Batch, sequences, and CLI.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Native GPU Engine</dt>
        <dd><strong>Metal, DirectX 12, Vulkan</strong> on macOS, Windows, and Linux—plus iPhone &amp; iPad soon. Free under GPL-3.0.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="features-highlights" aria-label="Highlights">
    <section class="feature-row" id="highlight-methods">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Three stacking methods</h2>
        <p>
          Different subjects respond best to different algorithms. Deptheos includes Pyramid, Depth, and Wavelet so
          you can pick the right method for each bracket—or stack with two and blend the best parts of each during
          retouching.
        </p>
        <p>All three compute in 32-bit floating point with no artificial sharpening or contrast added afterward.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/docs/methods/">Choosing a method</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="method-cards">
        <a class="method-card" href="/docs/pyramid/">
          <strong>Pyramid <span class="tag">default</span></strong>
          <p>Blends detail across multiple scales to keep crossing hairs, bristles, and antennae crisp and separate. The best all-around starting point.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="method-card" href="/docs/depth-map/">
          <strong>Depth</strong>
          <p>Builds a depth map to preserve smooth gradients, plain out-of-focus backgrounds, and natural color with minimal halos on high-contrast edges.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="method-card" href="/docs/wavelet/">
          <strong>Wavelet</strong>
          <p>Selects the strongest detail at each scale while taking each pixel's color whole from a single frame. Ideal for fine repeating textures and micro-detail.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="feature-row" id="highlight-align">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Automatic alignment</h2>
        <p>
          Focus breathing changes magnification between shots, and handheld brackets shift and rotate. ECC, the
          default aligner, aligns each frame to the previous one for shift, rotation, scale, stretch, and slant to a
          fraction of a pixel—with no settings required.
        </p>
        <p>
          For brackets with larger jumps between shots, Grid search lets you set explicit shift, scale, and rotation
          limits plus exposure compensation. Shoot near to far or far to near; frames that cannot be aligned are
          skipped and reported so the stack completes cleanly.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/docs/alignment/">Alignment</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot"><Shot name="stacking-frames" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Stacking workspace with 66 frames loaded and ECC affine alignment configured in the inspector." /><figcaption>Automatic ECC affine alignment in the Stacking workspace.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section class="feature-row" id="highlight-raw">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>RAW in, linear DNG out</h2>
        <p>
          RAW frames are stacked in linear camera RGB, before any tone curve, in 32-bit floating point. The result
          exports as a 16-bit linear DNG that Lightroom, Camera Raw, and other RAW editors open like a RAW file,
          with white balance, highlights, and shadows intact.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deptheos decodes camera RAW files with built-in LibRaw and writes linear DNGs natively on all desktop
          platforms. Adobe DNG Converter is also supported as an optional second decoder on macOS and Windows.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/docs/raw/">RAW, DNG and color</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot"><Shot name="export-dng" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Export sheet over a RAW stack with 16-bit linear DNG chosen." /><figcaption>Exporting a RAW stack as a 16-bit linear DNG.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section class="feature-row" id="highlight-retouch">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Retouching</h2>
        <p>
          Paint clean detail onto the stacked result from any source frame, or from another render of the same
          stack—for example, painting a Depth render's smooth background onto a Pyramid render. Option-click
          (Alt-click) any spot to jump to the sharpest frame there, with a live preview inside the brush.
        </p>
        <p>
          Adjust Size, Hardness, Opacity, and Flow, or enable <em>Only where colors match</em> to protect
          neighboring edges. The Eraser paints the original stacked result back, and all retouching is saved
          non-destructively with the render.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/docs/retouching/">Retouching</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot"><Shot name="paint-edge" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Retouch at 100% on the hornet with brush settings in the inspector." /><figcaption>Retouching from a source frame at 100%.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section class="feature-row" id="highlight-compare">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Viewing and comparison</h2>
        <p>
          Inspect results at 100% with Split view, dragging the divider between two renders or between a render and
          any aligned source frame, locked to one pan and zoom. Switch to Side by Side, Top and Bottom, or Flicker
          at any time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Optional Depth Map and Contribution views show which frame supplied each area of the image when you need
          to diagnose a tricky region.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/docs/compare/">Viewing and comparing</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot"><Shot name="compare-contribution" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Side by Side view with Contribution showing the pixels supplied by one frame." /><figcaption>Side by Side comparison with Contribution view.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section class="feature-row" id="highlight-batch">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Batch processing, sequences, and CLI</h2>
        <p>
          Batch stacks one bracket per folder—or automatically splits a folder of mixed shots into separate stacks by
          capture time—and exports each as it finishes. Any stack that fails is kept with its error so you can open
          and retry it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Export numbered frames for turntable sequences (one stack per angle) or grow sequences (the subject coming
          into focus across the bracket).
        </p>
        <p>
          The <code>deptheos</code> command-line tool runs stacking, batch, and sequence workflows from a terminal,
          exports aligned frames with <code>deptheos align</code>, and benchmarks your hardware with
          <code>deptheos bench</code>.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/docs/batch/">Batch</a> · <a href="/docs/cli/">Command line</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot">
        <video class="turntable-video" controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster="/media/turntable/pcb-poster.jpg" width="1920" height="1080" aria-label="A circuit board turning in full focus: a turntable sequence stacked in Deptheos">
          <source src="/media/turntable/pcb.av1.mp4" type='video/mp4; codecs="av01.0.08M.08"' />
          <source src="/media/turntable/pcb.h264.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
        <figcaption>A turntable sequence: one focus stack per angle, exported as numbered frames.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </section>

  <header class="features-full-head" id="all">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
    <h2>Everything, by area</h2>
  </header>
  <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-h">
    <h2 id="toc-h">On this page</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#stacking">Stacking methods</a></li>
